import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'1.1'))
from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('7.2')
for l,rows in {
'01':['Employment patterns can change across sectors, occupations, locations and working arrangements while the total level also changes.','A sector’s employment share can fall even if its number employed rises when total employment grows faster.','A simple unemployment rate uses unemployed people divided by the labour force, not the entire population.'],
'02':['Unemployment concerns people without work who are willing and able to work and seeking employment under the stated measure.','Unemployment can arise through weak demand, structural skill or location mismatch, seasonal activity and job-search friction.','Technological change can displace particular tasks while also creating different employment opportunities.'],
'03':['Unemployment can reduce household earned income, unused productive capacity and government tax receipts, with effects depending on other resources and support.','Longer unemployment can weaken skills, confidence and later employment prospects.','Employment loss at one firm does not by itself establish the same net economy-wide loss.'],
'04':['Training and mobility support address structural mismatch; demand support can address unemployment caused by weak spending when resources can respond.','Matching information can reduce search unemployment, while seasonal diversification may improve the availability of work across the year.','Remedy effectiveness depends on matching the cause, feasibility, cost and time rather than assuming one measure solves all unemployment.']}.items():
 for text in rows:t.claim(l,text)
t.block('01','plain_explanation','Describe levels and patterns separately',"""
Employment is work used in producing goods and services. Its level is the number employed, while its pattern concerns how jobs are distributed across sectors, occupations, places and working arrangements. Growth in services can coexist with declining manufacturing employment, or both can grow at different rates. A statement about one share does not establish the direction of the total.

Use invented employment figures. In Year 1 agriculture employs 200 people and services 300, giving 500 in total. In Year 2 agriculture employs 220 and services 380, giving 600. Agricultural employment rises by 20, yet its share falls from 40% to about 36.67%. Services grow faster. Describing agriculture as losing jobs because its share fell would confuse the relative pattern with the absolute level.

Changing customer demand, technology and skills can change occupational requirements. More machines can reduce demand for a manual task and raise demand for maintenance skills. The net job effect depends on those changes and the output sold. Full-time, part-time and self-employed work can also change differently, so a headcount alone may hide a change in hours or security.

A simple unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the labour force, employed plus unemployed under the specified definition. If 600 are employed and 100 unemployed, the labour force is 700 and the rate about 14.29%. Children and people outside the labour force are not added to the denominator merely because they are residents. Use a consistent measure before comparing periods, since changed definitions or search behaviour can affect the figures.
""")
t.block('02','plain_explanation','Identify the cause rather than assume unwillingness',"""
Unemployment concerns people without work who are willing and able to work and seeking employment under the stated measure. A person studying full-time without seeking work is not automatically unemployed. Nor does unemployment alone show unwillingness to work: an employer may have no vacancy, or available jobs may require different skills. The relevant evidence is the labour-market situation.

Cyclical or demand-deficient unemployment can follow a broad fall in orders. Firms reduce output and labour demand even when workers have useful skills. Structural unemployment arises when the pattern of jobs changes and workers’ skills or location do not match available vacancies. Technological unemployment is one possible structural case when equipment replaces particular tasks and displaced workers cannot readily fill new roles.

Seasonal unemployment arises when work is available only during part of the year, such as a supplied harvesting period. Frictional unemployment reflects time spent finding and moving into a suitable job. These cases need different explanations: a search delay differs from a permanent skill mismatch or a broad absence of buyers. More than one cause can affect the same person.

For example, an invented packing plant loses orders during a general spending decline and dismisses workers who could perform the former tasks. Demand support addresses a different problem from retraining a worker whose former task has permanently changed while suitable new vacancies exist. Neither case is diagnosed from the unemployment label alone. Name the initiating change and the reason it reduces access to paid work.
""")
t.block('03','plain_explanation','Follow the consequences to households and the economy',"""
Losing a job can reduce earned income and the ability to meet household spending commitments. Existing savings, another household income or support can cushion the immediate effect, but they are not guaranteed or unlimited. A household may reduce discretionary purchases first, while essential bills remain difficult to adjust. Reduced purchases can also weaken orders at other businesses if the spending loss is not offset elsewhere.

Unused willing and suitable labour represents potential output forgone when productive opportunities exist. Tax revenue may fall as earnings and spending decline, while support needs can increase. The fiscal effect depends on the tax and benefit arrangements supplied; no automatic payment or exact current amount is assumed. These are economy-wide channels rather than simply a cost shown in one employer’s accounts.

Long unemployment may allow skills to deteriorate or make it harder to maintain contacts and confidence. This can weaken later recruitment prospects, reinforcing the original loss. Short search unemployment need not have the same lasting effect. Duration and access to training matter when comparing consequences.

An invented closure removing 30 jobs at one plant is a gross local loss. If other businesses employ 20 of the affected workers during the same period, the net position differs from assuming all 30 remain unemployed. Relocation can still impose costs and the replacement jobs may differ in hours or pay. Assess both the net numbers and the individual circumstances rather than assuming a job replacement removes every consequence.
""")
t.block('04','evaluation','Match the remedy to the binding cause',"""
Training can help workers meet actual vacancies requiring new skills. It is more convincing when the course is relevant, affordable and accessible and employers genuinely need the skill. Training takes time and does not create customers by itself. If most firms face weak demand, newly qualified workers may still lack vacancies. Job information and placement support can instead reduce search delays where suitable vacancies already exist.

Demand support, such as feasible funded public purchases, can help where suitable workers and capacity are idle because orders are weak. It may create output and jobs if resources respond. Its limits include financing, implementation delays, imports and price pressure when capacity is tight. Topic 6.2 explains those policy channels; the present task is matching them to the unemployment cause.

Mobility support can help where vacancies are elsewhere, but housing, transport and family commitments affect feasibility. Occupational mobility can require training; geographical mobility concerns location. Seasonal diversification can provide work outside the main production season if demand for the alternative output exists. A calendar change does not itself create that demand.

Evaluate a remedy through cause, mechanism, cost and time. For a skill mismatch with unfilled vacancies, relevant training is well targeted. For a general fall in demand with unchanged useful skills, demand support is more direct, provided it is affordable and capacity can respond. A combination may be needed where both problems exist. A conditional choice grounded in the actual constraint is stronger than declaring one remedy best for all unemployed people.

Check additionality when counting the benefit. A programme hiring 50 people may replace existing work or draw some recruits from other employers. Its gross places are not automatically 50 extra jobs for the economy. Similarly, a completed course is not itself evidence of later employment. Relevant follow-up would compare actual placement into sustained suitable work, including the time needed to obtain it.

Measures can also complement each other. Information about genuine vacancies can help a trained worker find a match, while affordable transport can make that match feasible. Where those barriers coexist, improving skills alone may leave the last obstacle intact. Diagnose the sequence of constraints before deciding which resources to spend.

The relevant comparison is sustained suitable employment, including the actual hours and earnings, rather than course attendance or announced vacancies alone.
""")
def s(n):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['slot'].split('-')[-2]==f'{n:03}')
def c(n,l,p,a,g):t.card(s(n),l,p,a,g)
g=['Starting change (1): relevant demand, sector or employment change. Because/mechanism (2): develop two connected steps using the facts. Consequence (1): resulting employment pattern or cost, with conditions.']
c(1,'01','Explain how agricultural employment can rise while its share falls using invented totals: Year 1 agriculture 200/services 300; Year 2 agriculture 220/services 380.','Agriculture rises 20, but total employment rises 500 to 600 as services grow faster. Agriculture’s share falls from 40% to about 36.67%; its level and share therefore move differently.',g)
c(2,'01','Explain the denominator for an invented unemployment rate with 600 employed, 100 unemployed under the stated definition and 300 residents outside the labour force.','The labour force is 600 + 100 = 700, so the rate is 100/700, about 14.29%. The 300 outside the labour force are excluded.',['Context (1): employed plus unemployed denominator. Reason/conclusion (1): rate about 14.29%, excluding others.'])
c(3,'02','Explain unemployment where a broad fall in orders leads firms to cut output and dismiss workers whose skills remain suitable.','Reduced demand for products reduces the output firms plan to sell. Their derived demand for labour falls, creating demand-deficient unemployment despite suitable worker skills.',g)
c(4,'02','Explain the unemployment cause where new technician vacancies exist but displaced manual workers lack the required skill.','This is structural skill mismatch. Vacancies do not provide employment to those workers immediately because their skills do not meet the new job requirements.',['Context (1): vacancies and unsuitable skills. Reason/conclusion (1): structural mismatch limits access.'])
c(5,'03','Explain how prolonged unemployment can affect a household with no replacement earnings and limited savings.','Lost earnings reduce available spending funds. Limited savings can cushion only some costs; continued unemployment can force cuts and hardship and may weaken skills or recruitment prospects over time.',g)
c(6,'03','Explain why closure of a plant with 30 jobs is not evidence that all 30 remain unemployed when 20 affected workers find other work in the same period.','The closure is a gross job loss, while subsequent employment offsets part of it. The net unemployment effect differs, although replacement pay, hours and transition costs still matter.',['Context (1): re-employment of 20. Reason/conclusion (1): distinguish gross loss from net unemployment.'])
c(7,'04','Evaluate training as the main remedy where unemployed workers lack the skills for genuine unfilled vacancies, but courses take time and some workers cannot easily attend.','Relevant training addresses the mismatch and can improve access to vacancies. Time and attendance barriers limit the response, and jobs must remain available. Support targeted accessible training under those conditions; broad demand support alone does not supply the missing skill.',['Argument for (2): skill acquired (1), access to genuine jobs (1). Argument against (2): time (1), attendance barrier (1). Condition (1): relevant accessible training and sustained vacancies. Judgement (2): targeted training supported (1), matched to cause (1). Total 7.'])
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][0],'02 03 04','Define unemployment (2). Identify causes in two invented cases: broad weak orders with suitable workers; vacancies requiring skills workers lack (2). Explain one consequence of lost work where there is no replacement income (2). Identify a remedy matched to each of those causes (2).','Unemployment concerns people without work, willing and able to work and seeking employment under the measure. The causes are demand deficiency and structural mismatch. Lost earnings reduce purchasing power. Feasible demand support addresses weak orders; relevant training addresses the skill gap.',['Meaning (2): without work (1), willing/able/seeking under measure (1). Separate points for causes (2): demand deficiency (1), structural mismatch (1). Explanation (2): earnings lost (1), reduced spending capacity (1). Remedies (2): demand support (1), relevant training (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
base='Invented district: total employment rises 500 to 600, with agriculture 200 to 220 and services 300 to 380. Group A workers lose jobs after a broad fall in orders despite suitable skills and spare capacity. Group B cannot fill genuine technician vacancies because training is missing. Both groups lose earned income; savings are limited. Funded demand support and relevant accessible training are feasible, but each uses resources and training takes time.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01 02 03 04',base+' Describe three employment/cause features, each with a detail (6). Explain the distinct unemployment mechanisms and consequences for earnings and spending (7). Discuss demand support versus training for these groups (7).','Agricultural jobs rise but share falls; A has demand-deficient unemployment; B has structural mismatch. Weak orders reduce output and labour demand for A, while missing skills prevent B accessing existing vacancies. Lost earnings reduce spending where savings are limited. Demand support is more direct for A, training for B; cost, time and sustained suitable vacancies condition the combination.',['Data description (6): changing agriculture share/level (1), 200 to 220 with faster total growth detail (1); demand cause A (1), weak orders detail (1); mismatch B (1), missing technician skill detail (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): weak orders (1), output cut (1), labour demand falls (1); vacancies exist (1), B lacks required skill (1); earnings lost (1), limited savings weaken spending (1). Both sides/judgement (7): demand support creates A orders (1), training equips B (1); demand alone not B skill solution (1), training alone not A customer solution (1); condition cost/time and actual capacity/vacancies (1); judgement matched combination (1), group-specific reason (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
cases='Invented cases. Case 1: genuine technician vacancies exist, but unemployed workers lack the relevant skill; accessible funded training takes six months. Case 2: a general demand fall has reduced orders, while unemployed workers retain suitable skills and capacity is idle; funded purchases can start, but imports absorb part of spending. No general skill shortage is stated in Case 2.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][2],'04',cases+' Evaluate relevant training versus demand support in Case 1 (7). Evaluate those remedies in Case 2 (7).','Case 1: training directly fills the skill gap, though six months delays employment and vacancies must persist; demand support alone does not create the qualification. Case 2: purchases directly support weak orders and use idle capacity, though imports and funding limit the effect; training does not directly repair the stated lack of demand. Choose the remedy matching each cause with its implementation conditions.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for skill remedy (1), existing vacancies (1); argument against six-month delay (1), vacancy persistence uncertain (1); condition relevant successful training (1); judgement training priority (1), demand alone not skill solution (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for orders (1), idle resources respond (1); argument against import content (1), funding/implementation costs (1); condition domestic response and affordability (1); judgement demand support priority (1), training alone not stated cause remedy (1). Total 14.'],[cases])
t.finish()
